{
  "abstract": "Two results of the manuscript \"Low-discrepancy quasi-uniform digital sequences in every dimension\" (version 1): Corollary cor:halton of Theorem thm:nonqu, and Theorem thm:residue with Corollaries cor:korobov and cor:residueQU for one explicit sequence. Quasi-uniform means here: there is a real B such that h(P_[N]) <= B delta(P_[N]) for every N >= 2, where P_[N] is the set of the first N points, h is the Euclidean covering radius of [0,1]^s (a supremum over the cube of the minimum distance to the points) and delta is the minimum Euclidean distance over pairs of distinct indices. (1) For every prime p, every s >= 2 and all pairwise coprime polynomials b_0, ..., b_(s-1) over Z/pZ of degree >= 1, the Halton-type sequence in these bases (Goda-Hofer-Suzuki, Def. 2: coordinate c of the point with index n = sum_k n_k p^k reads the b_c-adic digits (V div b_c^Q) mod b_c of V = sum_k n_k z^k, each written as its deg(b_c) coefficients, the coefficient of z^(deg(b_c)-1) first) is not quasi-uniform, and for every eps > 0 there are m and two distinct indices v, w < p^m whose points have Euclidean distance less than eps (p^m)^(-1/s). For every prime p and every s such bases exist. (2) Let s >= 2, let p > 2^s be prime and rho = floor(p^(1/s)). Consider the residue sequence with the Faure places a_c = c, scale theta = 1 and the Korobov check vector v = (rho, rho^2, ..., rho^(s-1), -1): the digital sequence over Z/pZ with generator matrices C_c[n][i] = v_c^(-1) [t^n] (c + t)^i w_c(c + t)^(-(n+1)), w_c(z) = prod_(c' != c) (z - c'), and coordinates y_c = sum_n y_(c,n) p^(-n-1) with digits in {0, ..., p-1}. It is a (0,s)-sequence in base p; for every K >= 1, any two distinct indices u, u' < N = p^(sK-1) give points at toroidal Euclidean distance (sum_c (d_c - round(d_c))^2)^(1/2), d = difference of the points, at least (rho - 1) p^(-1/s) N^(-1/s); and it is quasi-uniform. Moreover rho >= 2 and rho^s < p, every v_c is nonzero, the places are distinct, and the power series w_c(c + t) over Z/pZ times its inverse is 1. (The entry states no discrepancy bound; that (0,s)-sequences have low discrepancy is classical and is not part of this entry.)\nComparator configuration: comparatorHeadlineSeqV4.json (4 compared theorems, all proved, no sorry): GeoSeqV4.halton_not_quasiUniform, GeoSeqV4.halton_bases_exist, GeoSeqV4.residue_quasiUniform, GeoSeqV4.residue_data. The Challenge (HeadlineSeqV4/Challenge.lean, 195 lines) imports only Mathlib and defines everything it uses from scratch (digital sequences from infinite generator matrices over ZMod p, the (0,s)-sequence property, Euclidean and toroidal distances, covering radius, minimum distance, quasi-uniformity, the Halton-type points, the residue generator matrices). The Solution (HeadlineSeqV4/Solution.lean) imports a verbatim copy of these definitions (HeadlineSeqV4/Defs.lean, generated by scripts/gen_headline_seq_v4_defs.py) and the full formalization GeoSeq of the same Lake project, proves bridge lemmas (each definition equals the corresponding GeoSeq definition) and obtains the theorems from compared theorems of GeoSeq (configurations comparatorGeoSeqV9.json, comparatorGeoSeqV93.json).",
